//? Title: Hilbert-Schmidt independence criterion
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Test whether two or more random vectors are (jointly) independent with kernel embeddings, and see how the kernel bandwidth decides which dependence the test can see

The **Hilbert-Schmidt independence criterion** (Gretton et al. 2005) measures dependence as the distance between the kernel mean embeddings of the joint distribution and of the product of the marginals. With a characteristic kernel, such as the Gaussian \(k(x,x')=\exp(-\lVert x-x'\rVert^2/2\sigma^2)\), it is zero only under independence. For Gram matrices \(K\) and \(L\), the V-statistic is

\[\widehat{\mathrm{HSIC}}=\frac1{n^2}\sum_{i,j}K_{ij}L_{ij}+\frac1{n^4}\sum_{i,j}K_{ij}\sum_{k,l}L_{kl}-\frac2{n^3}\sum_{i}\Bigl(\sum_jK_{ij}\Bigr)\Bigl(\sum_kL_{ik}\Bigr).\]

**dHSIC** (Pfister et al. 2018) extends this to \(d\) variables and tests *joint* independence \(P_{X_1\cdots X_d}=P_{X_1}\otimes\cdots\otimes P_{X_d}\), which is stronger than pairwise independence. Two tests are available: a permutation test, which permutes variables \(2,\dots,d\) independently and is exact, and a gamma approximation to the null distribution of \(n\,\widehat{\mathrm{dHSIC}}\) with matched mean and variance, which is fast. Distance covariance is HSIC with a particular (distance-induced) kernel (Sejdinovic et al. 2013). HSIC's Gaussian kernel adds a scale: the bandwidth \(\sigma\), by default set by the median heuristic \(\sigma^2=\operatorname{median}\lVert x_i-x_j\rVert^2/2\).

`hsic` takes any number of variables, each an \(n\times p_j\) matrix, with Gaussian kernels (median heuristic, optionally rescaled by `bandwidth_scale`, or a fixed bandwidth) or a discrete kernel for categorical data. It returns the statistic, the bandwidths, the gamma test and an optional permutation test. The tests reproduce `dHSIC` 2.1 (statistics, bandwidths and gamma p-values for two and three variables, fixed and discrete kernels) to \(10^{-9}\) relative, and its permutation p-values within Monte Carlo error.

The example builds three variables that are pairwise independent but jointly dependent. The simulations check the size of the gamma and permutation tests, including for heavy-tailed data, and trace power against the bandwidth multiplier for a smooth signal and an oscillating one, with distance correlation as a benchmark.

```{cpp, id=hs, file="figures/hsic.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/random/random.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/distance_correlation.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/hsic.hpp>

#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <numbers>
#include <print>
#include <vector>

namespace dm = datamunge;
using Matrix = dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double>;

Matrix column(const std::vector<double>& v) {
    Matrix m(v.size(), 1, 0.0);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < v.size(); ++i) m(i, 0) = v[i];
    return m;
}

int main() {
    dm::random::SplitMix64 rng(31);

    // X, Y independent normals; Z = sign(XY)|E|. Pairs are independent, the triple is not.
    const std::size_t n = 200;
    std::vector<double> x(n), y(n), z(n);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        x[i] = rng.normal(), y[i] = rng.normal();
        z[i] = (x[i] * y[i] > 0 ? 1 : -1) * std::abs(rng.normal());
    }
    const dm::stats::HsicOptions perm{.permutations = 1999, .seed = 2};
    std::println("| Hypothesis (n = 200) | n x dHSIC | Gamma p | Permutation p |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    const auto show = [&](const char* name, const std::vector<Matrix>& vars) {
        const auto h = dm::stats::hsic(vars, perm);
        std::println("| {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} |", name, h.scaled_statistic,
                     h.gamma_p_value, h.permutation_p_value);
    };
    show("X independent of Y", {column(x), column(y)});
    show("X independent of Z", {column(x), column(z)});
    show("Y independent of Z", {column(y), column(z)});
    show("X, Y, Z jointly independent", {column(x), column(y), column(z)});
    Matrix xy(n, 2, 0.0);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) xy(i, 0) = x[i], xy(i, 1) = y[i];
    show("(X, Y) independent of Z", {xy, column(z)});

    // Size under independence: gamma approximation versus permutation.
    std::println("\n| Independent data | n | Gamma test size | Permutation test size |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (const bool cauchy : {false, true})
        for (const std::size_t m : {30uz, 100uz}) {
            const int reps = 1000;
            double g = 0, p = 0;
            for (int r = 0; r < reps; ++r) {
                std::vector<double> a(m), b(m);
                for (std::size_t i = 0; i < m; ++i) {
                    a[i] = cauchy ? std::tan(std::numbers::pi * (rng.uniform01() - 0.5))
                                  : rng.normal();
                    b[i] = cauchy ? std::tan(std::numbers::pi * (rng.uniform01() - 0.5))
                                  : rng.normal();
                }
                const auto h = dm::stats::hsic(
                    a, b, {.permutations = 199, .seed = static_cast<std::uint64_t>(r + 1)});
                g += (h.gamma_p_value <= 0.05) / double(reps);
                p += (h.permutation_p_value <= 0.05) / double(reps);
            }
            std::println("| {} | {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", cauchy ? "Cauchy" : "Normal", m,
                         g, p);
        }

    // Power against bandwidth multiplier, n = 100, gamma test at 5%.
    const std::vector<double> scales{0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.35, 0.5, 1, 2, 4, 8};
    const int reps = 400;
    std::vector<std::vector<double>> power(2, std::vector<double>(scales.size(), 0.0));
    double dcor_power[2] = {0, 0};
    for (int signal = 0; signal < 2; ++signal)
        for (int r = 0; r < reps; ++r) {
            std::vector<double> a(100), b(100);
            for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 100; ++i) {
                a[i] = 2 * rng.uniform01() - 1;
                const double e = rng.normal();
                b[i] = signal == 0 ? a[i] * a[i] + 0.4 * e
                                   : std::sin(6 * std::numbers::pi * a[i]) + 0.5 * e;
            }
            for (std::size_t s = 0; s < scales.size(); ++s) {
                const auto h = dm::stats::hsic(a, b, {.bandwidth_scale = scales[s]});
                power[signal][s] += (h.gamma_p_value <= 0.05) / double(reps);
            }
            const auto d = dm::stats::distance_correlation(
                a, b, {.permutations = 199, .seed = static_cast<std::uint64_t>(r + 1)});
            dcor_power[signal] += (d.permutation_p_value <= 0.05) / double(reps);
        }
    std::println("\n| Bandwidth / median heuristic | Quadratic: HSIC power | "
                 "Sine (3 periods): HSIC power |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (std::size_t s = 0; s < scales.size(); ++s)
        std::println("| {:.2f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", scales[s], power[0][s], power[1][s]);
    std::println("| Distance correlation (permutation) | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", dcor_power[0],
                 dcor_power[1]);

    std::vector<double> logs;
    for (const double s : scales) logs.push_back(std::log2(s));
    auto fig = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    fig.line(logs, power[0], "Quadratic, HSIC", {176, 72, 43}, 3)
        .line(logs, power[1], "Sine, HSIC", {26, 90, 147}, 3)
        .line({logs.front(), logs.back()}, {dcor_power[0], dcor_power[0]},
              "Quadratic, dCor", {232, 165, 135}, 2)
        .line({logs.front(), logs.back()}, {dcor_power[1], dcor_power[1]}, "Sine, dCor",
              {140, 182, 222}, 2)
        .abline_v(0, {170, 170, 170})
        .abline_h(0.05, {170, 170, 170})
        .y_limits(0, 1.3)
        .title("HSIC power depends on the kernel bandwidth (n = 100)")
        .x_label("log2(bandwidth / median heuristic)")
        .y_label("Rejection rate at 5%")
        .size(640, 410);
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    fig.save_svg("build/hsic.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Hypothesis (n = 200) | n x dHSIC | Gamma p | Permutation p |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| X independent of Y | 0.5064 | 0.1029 | 0.1010 |
| X independent of Z | 0.1929 | 0.8179 | 0.8465 |
| Y independent of Z | 0.2300 | 0.7198 | 0.7330 |
| X, Y, Z jointly independent | 2.1446 | 0.0000 | 0.0005 |
| (X, Y) independent of Z | 1.3546 | 0.0000 | 0.0005 |

| Independent data | n | Gamma test size | Permutation test size |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Normal | 30 | 0.057 | 0.054 |
| Normal | 100 | 0.060 | 0.055 |
| Cauchy | 30 | 0.064 | 0.060 |
| Cauchy | 100 | 0.065 | 0.061 |

| Bandwidth / median heuristic | Quadratic: HSIC power | Sine (3 periods): HSIC power |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.05 | 0.085 | 0.350 |
| 0.10 | 0.283 | 0.667 |
| 0.20 | 0.645 | 0.572 |
| 0.35 | 0.920 | 0.145 |
| 0.50 | 0.967 | 0.103 |
| 1.00 | 0.995 | 0.118 |
| 2.00 | 0.985 | 0.150 |
| 4.00 | 0.320 | 0.195 |
| 8.00 | 0.110 | 0.200 |
| Distance correlation (permutation) | 0.982 | 0.205 |
\image(figures/hsic.svg)
// result_end

The first table is the case dHSIC was built for. Each pair among X, Y and Z is independent (Z's sign is a fair coin given either X or Y alone), and the pairwise tests give p = 0.10, 0.82 and 0.73. Yet Z is a deterministic function of the signs of X and Y together, and the joint test rejects with p < 0.001, as does the test of (X, Y) against Z. Pairwise screening, with any dependence measure, cannot find such structure. Only a test of joint independence can.

Both tests hold their level well. The permutation test is exact up to Monte Carlo error (5.4–6.1% in 1000 replicates), and the gamma approximation is only slightly liberal (5.7–6.5%), even for Cauchy data, because the Gaussian kernel is bounded and needs no moments. Distance correlation, by contrast, requires finite first moments.

The bandwidth decides what HSIC sees. For the smooth quadratic signal, power is above 92% for bandwidths from 0.35 to 2 times the median heuristic, which is itself near-optimal (99.5%, matching distance correlation's 98%). Very small bandwidths make the kernel nearly an indicator of identical points. Very large ones reduce it to \(1-\lVert x-x'\rVert^2/2\sigma^2\), where HSIC tends to squared linear covariance and the symmetric quadratic vanishes (11% power at 8×).

The oscillating sine is the opposite case. Its three periods are local structure that the median bandwidth averages away (12% power). A bandwidth of a tenth of the median, comparable to a half-period, raises power to 67%, while distance correlation, which has no scale parameter, detects it in only 20% of samples. No single bandwidth is best for both signals.

>> Practical guidance

- Use dHSIC to test joint independence of several variables, for example the residuals of a causal model, or the sources in independent component analysis. Pairwise tests can miss higher-order dependence.
- The median heuristic is a sound default for smooth dependence. If structure at smaller scales is plausible, fix the bandwidth or the multiplier before seeing the data. Choosing the bandwidth that gives the smallest p-value invalidates the test; if several bandwidths are tried, combine them with a Bonferroni or max-type permutation test.
- Use the gamma approximation for screening and large studies, and the permutation test (999 or more replicates) for reported p-values.
- Use the discrete kernel for categorical variables. It lets HSIC test mixed categorical and continuous data on the same footing.
- Gram matrices take O(n²) memory and time. For n beyond a few thousand, use random Fourier features or incomplete Cholesky approximations.

>> References

- A. Gretton, O. Bousquet, A. Smola and B. Schölkopf (2005). Measuring statistical dependence with Hilbert-Schmidt norms. *Algorithmic Learning Theory*, LNCS 3734, 63-77. doi:10.1007/11564089_7
- A. Gretton, K. Fukumizu, C. H. Teo, L. Song, B. Schölkopf and A. Smola (2008). A kernel statistical test of independence. *Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems* 20, 585-592.
- N. Pfister, P. Bühlmann, B. Schölkopf and J. Peters (2018). Kernel-based tests for joint independence. *Journal of the Royal Statistical Society B* 80(1), 5-31. doi:10.1111/rssb.12235
- D. Sejdinovic, B. Sriperumbudur, A. Gretton and K. Fukumizu (2013). Equivalence of distance-based and RKHS-based statistics in hypothesis testing. *Annals of Statistics* 41(5), 2263-2291. doi:10.1214/13-AOS1140
